WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.944 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING][RUSTLING][CLICKING]

00:00:03.944 --> 00:00:18.760 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:25.020 align:middle line:84%
CASEY RODRIGUEZ: OK, so
the name of the game today

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.520 align:middle line:90%
is compactness.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:33.000 align:middle line:90%


00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.360 align:middle line:84%
And so last time I recalled
that the definition

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:48.390 align:middle line:84%
of a subset of A metric
space x is compact

00:00:48.390 --> 00:01:04.080 align:middle line:84%
if every sequence xn in k, so
every sequence of elements in k

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:14.000 align:middle line:84%
has a subsequence
converging in K,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.230 align:middle line:84%
has a subsequence which
converges to an element in k.

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:24.290 align:middle line:84%
And so for example by
the Heiner-Borel theorem,

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:29.510 align:middle line:84%
closed and bounded subsets
of Rn or Cn are compact.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:36.280 align:middle line:90%
This is from 18100.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.520 align:middle line:84%
And general closed and bounded
subsets of a Hilbert space

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:41.720 align:middle line:90%
are not necessarily compact.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:48.470 align:middle line:84%
Last time we looked at the
closed unit ball in the Hilbert

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.380 align:middle line:84%
space, or let's
say in little l2,

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:55.490 align:middle line:84%
this is not compact because
you can take or orthonormal

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.680 align:middle line:84%
basis vectors which are the
sequences that have zero

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.900 align:middle line:90%
except for one in the n-th spot.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.270 align:middle line:90%
These are all of unit linked.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:10.910 align:middle line:84%
And the sequence consisting
of these basis vectors, which

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:12.950 align:middle line:84%
are sequences, so the
sequence of sequences

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:16.370 align:middle line:84%
does not converge in little
l2 or have a subsequence which

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.200 align:middle line:90%
converges in little l2.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.930 align:middle line:84%
So we were looking for
an additional condition

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.820 align:middle line:90%
that would ensure compactness.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.430 align:middle line:84%
And as a warm up
to this, last time

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:32.375 align:middle line:84%
we proved the
following that at--

00:02:32.375 --> 00:02:37.020 align:middle line:90%


00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.000 align:middle line:84%
so we're always working
in a Hilbert space that

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.665 align:middle line:84%
may be separable or not
depending on if I say it is

00:02:41.665 --> 00:02:42.165 align:middle line:90%
or not--

00:02:42.165 --> 00:02:45.930 align:middle line:90%


00:02:45.930 --> 00:03:05.190 align:middle line:84%
that if we take a sequence,
a convergent sequence in H,

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:10.710 align:middle line:84%
then the conclusion is, 1,
that the set k consisting

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:19.680 align:middle line:84%
of all the elements of
this sequence and the limit

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:20.445 align:middle line:90%
is compact.

00:03:20.445 --> 00:03:23.280 align:middle line:90%


00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:30.060 align:middle line:84%
And the set k has what are
called equi-small tails with

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:37.060 align:middle line:84%
respect to any
orthonormal subset of H.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:55.370 align:middle line:84%
If e sub k is a countable
orthonormal subset of H,

00:03:55.370 --> 00:04:00.380 align:middle line:84%
then the following holds, this
condition we called K having

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:06.330 align:middle line:84%
equi-small tails, then for
every epsilon positive,

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:12.770 align:middle line:84%
there exists a natural
number N such that for all--

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:23.090 align:middle line:84%
let's call it-- use a
different letter, v tilde in K,

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.900 align:middle line:84%
so either an element
of the sequence or v,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:39.900 align:middle line:84%
we have that the sum over K
bigger than N squared is less

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.710 align:middle line:90%
than epsilon squared.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:43.470 align:middle line:84%
So what this says
in this condition

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:53.950 align:middle line:84%
we called K has equi-small
tails with respect

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.480 align:middle line:90%
to this orthonormal subset.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:02.050 align:middle line:84%
So of course for any fixed
v, because this entire some--

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.210 align:middle line:84%
forget just K over
N-- is bounded

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.620 align:middle line:84%
by the norm of this v tilde
squared by Bessel's inequality.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:14.360 align:middle line:84%
We can always choose an N
for any individual v tilde.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.120 align:middle line:84%
Now equi-small
tails means that I

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:22.190 align:middle line:84%
can choose this independent
of the element in K.

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:24.950 align:middle line:84%
So if you like uniformly
small tails is another way

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:27.870 align:middle line:90%
you could think about it.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:32.300 align:middle line:84%
But the terminology equi
has been used instead.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.850 align:middle line:84%
But another way is to
think we can uniformly

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:39.740 align:middle line:84%
make the tails with respect
to an orthonormal sequence,

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:42.140 align:middle line:84%
or orthonormal
subset of H small.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:48.260 align:middle line:90%


00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:51.110 align:middle line:84%
Now, what we're
going to prove is

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:56.180 align:middle line:84%
that, in fact, this condition
here of having equi-small tails

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:01.100 align:middle line:84%
with respect to an orthonormal
basis on top of the set being

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:04.940 align:middle line:84%
closed and bounded,
suffice to prove

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.170 align:middle line:90%
that the subset is compact.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:11.890 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
following theorem.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:22.795 align:middle line:84%
So let H be a separable
Hilbert space.

00:06:22.795 --> 00:06:33.110 align:middle line:90%


00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:54.740 align:middle line:84%
And let ek be an orthonormal
basis of H. So I said suffice,

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.150 align:middle line:90%
but not just suffice.

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:58.160 align:middle line:84%
It's also necessary,
as we'll show.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:16.650 align:middle line:84%
Then K subset of H is compact
if and only if K is closed,

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:32.880 align:middle line:84%
bounded, and has equi-small
tails with respect

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.670 align:middle line:84%
to this orthonormal
basis, so in particular

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:39.855 align:middle line:90%
for any orthonormal basis.

00:07:39.855 --> 00:07:50.110 align:middle line:90%


00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:53.110 align:middle line:84%
So this is a
complete description

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.300 align:middle line:84%
of compact subsets
of a Hilbert space,

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:57.850 align:middle line:84%
it's those subsets which
are closed, bounded,

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:00.940 align:middle line:84%
and have equi-small
details with respect

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:05.170 align:middle line:90%
to one orthonormal basis.

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:06.910 align:middle line:84%
And what this
theorem also says is

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.520 align:middle line:84%
that if it has equi-small
details with respect

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.670 align:middle line:84%
to one orthonormal basis
and is closed and bounded,

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:15.265 align:middle line:84%
then the same holds with respect
to any orthonormal basis.

00:08:15.265 --> 00:08:18.630 align:middle line:90%


00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.460 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let's prove--

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:21.645 align:middle line:90%
so it's a two way street.

00:08:21.645 --> 00:08:26.830 align:middle line:90%


00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:30.550 align:middle line:84%
Well, it doesn't make sense
to say one street is shorter,

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:33.010 align:middle line:84%
one side of the
street is shorter--

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.740 align:middle line:90%
maybe wider, easier to use.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:37.870 align:middle line:84%
But let's prove that
if K is compact,

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.289 align:middle line:90%
then it implies all of this.

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:56.840 align:middle line:84%
So suppose K is compact
and K is closed and bounded

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.430 align:middle line:90%
by general metric space theory.

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:05.220 align:middle line:90%


00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:13.170 align:middle line:84%
So look this up in
the 18100's material,

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:16.950 align:middle line:90%
or intro to analysis material.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:27.130 align:middle line:84%
So let's-- this also gives us an
opportunity to understand this

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.960 align:middle line:84%
equi-small tail condition as
well by having to negate it.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.890 align:middle line:84%
So we're also going to prove
that K has equi-small details

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:34.310 align:middle line:90%
by contradiction.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:49.410 align:middle line:84%
So suppose K does not have
equi-small tails with respect

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:55.360 align:middle line:84%
to this basis, or this
orthonormal basis.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:58.130 align:middle line:90%
Then what's that mean?

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:02.520 align:middle line:84%
So this is a for all there
exists in a statement.

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:09.130 align:middle line:84%
So then there exists
some bad epsilon

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:28.280 align:middle line:84%
so that for all N, a natural
number, there exist a uN in K

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:29.840 align:middle line:90%
such that--

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:34.700 align:middle line:84%
so these N's are
increasing such that u--

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:38.090 align:middle line:84%
so we have the negation
of the condition that

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:46.070 align:middle line:84%
satisfied K bigger
than N of uN eK squared

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:48.425 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than or equal
to this epsilon 0 squared.

00:10:48.425 --> 00:11:00.690 align:middle line:90%


00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:10.200 align:middle line:84%
So note that this is a
sequence in this compact set K.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.560 align:middle line:84%
And so by the defining
property of a compact set,

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:20.350 align:middle line:84%
it must have a convergent
subsequence, which

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:35.580 align:middle line:84%
implies there exists
subsequence which you usually

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:40.320 align:middle line:84%
denote use of N, capital N
sub m, some other letter.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:45.646 align:middle line:84%
But I'll just call
it, to make it

00:11:45.646 --> 00:11:50.090 align:middle line:84%
a little connection back to what
we wrote a minute ago, v sub n.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:58.670 align:middle line:84%
So this is a subsequence of u
sub capital N and v in K such

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:11.660 align:middle line:84%
that v sub n converges to v. Now
then for all N, natural number,

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:22.070 align:middle line:84%
we have that sum over K
bigger than n of vN eK squared

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:27.230 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than epsilon
0 squared because this

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:30.080 align:middle line:84%
is a subsequence of the
original sequence which

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:31.430 align:middle line:90%
satisfied this condition.

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:35.660 align:middle line:90%


00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:38.450 align:middle line:84%
Now this would actually
be capital N sub n.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:41.150 align:middle line:84%
But if I throw in some
more, capital N sub n

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:45.990 align:middle line:84%
would be an increasing
sequence of numbers.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:47.240 align:middle line:90%
And it's always bigger than n.

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.790 align:middle line:84%
So I can replace that
capital N sub n with this.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:51.485 align:middle line:90%
All right, so this is fine.

00:12:51.485 --> 00:12:54.070 align:middle line:90%


00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:59.680 align:middle line:84%
Then that says the subset
consisting of the elements v

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:07.990 align:middle line:84%
sub n in the natural
number union v

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:13.110 align:middle line:84%
does not have
equi-small tails, which

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:17.760 align:middle line:84%
is a contradiction to the
theorem we proved last time.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.450 align:middle line:90%


00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:22.670 align:middle line:84%
This contradicts the previous
theorem that I stated

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:25.490 align:middle line:90%
and we proved last time.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:29.420 align:middle line:84%
So any convergent sequence along
with its limit is a compact set

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:32.160 align:middle line:90%
and has equi-small tails.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:36.240 align:middle line:90%
So that's a contradiction.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:41.520 align:middle line:84%
And what was the assumption that
brought us to this road forking

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:46.200 align:middle line:84%
itself is the assumption
that K is not compact.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:47.550 align:middle line:90%
So therefore K--

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:48.720 align:middle line:90%
I mean, not K's compact.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:59.840 align:middle line:84%
K has equi-small
tails with respect

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:03.690 align:middle line:90%
to this orthonormal basis.

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:08.570 align:middle line:84%
So that's one direction
that if K is compact then

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:10.828 align:middle line:84%
it has to have equi-small
tails with respect

00:14:10.828 --> 00:14:11.870 align:middle line:90%
to the orthonormal basis.

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:14.920 align:middle line:90%


00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.750 align:middle line:84%
We didn't use anything
about it being a basis,

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:21.760 align:middle line:84%
so this also says that if
you have a compact set, then

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:23.890 align:middle line:84%
with respect to any
orthonormal subset,

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:25.450 align:middle line:84%
it has equi-small
tails with respect

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:28.480 align:middle line:90%
to that orthonormal subset.

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:35.940 align:middle line:84%
So now let's use this condition
that K is closed and bounded

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:41.850 align:middle line:84%
and has equi-small tails
to prove that K is compact.

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:56.480 align:middle line:84%
So suppose K is closed,
bounded, and has

00:14:56.480 --> 00:15:07.860 align:middle line:84%
equi-small tails with respect
to this orthonormal basis.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:11.760 align:middle line:90%


00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:23.320 align:middle line:84%
Now let uN be a
sequence in K. So we

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:27.460 align:middle line:84%
want to show that it has a
subsequence which converges

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:30.280 align:middle line:84%
to an element in K.
Now, K is closed,

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:34.450 align:middle line:84%
which means that if I take any
sequence which converges in K,

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:37.790 align:middle line:84%
or which any sequence of
elements in K that converges,

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:40.390 align:middle line:84%
the limit has to be in
K. That's the definition

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:41.860 align:middle line:90%
of a set being closed.

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:45.320 align:middle line:84%
So I just need to show that
this sequence, in fact,

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:47.527 align:middle line:90%
has a convergence subsequence.

00:15:47.527 --> 00:15:48.610 align:middle line:90%
That's all I have to show.

00:15:48.610 --> 00:16:10.300 align:middle line:84%
So since K is closed,
we just need to show uN

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:15.840 align:middle line:90%
as a convergent subsequence.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:19.860 align:middle line:90%


00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:32.000 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is to use what
we know about bounded subsets

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:33.060 align:middle line:90%
of complex numbers.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:38.970 align:middle line:84%
So we know about bounded
subsets of complex numbers.

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:41.570 align:middle line:90%
If I have a bounded sequence--

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:43.880 align:middle line:84%
I shouldn't say
subsets, but if I

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:46.820 align:middle line:84%
have a bounded sequence
of complex numbers,

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.290 align:middle line:90%
it has a convergent subsequence.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:50.900 align:middle line:90%
This is a Bolzano-Weierstrass.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.420 align:middle line:84%
Usually it's stated in
terms of subsets of R.

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:58.170 align:middle line:84%
But sequences of complex
numbers converge if and only

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:01.260 align:middle line:84%
if the real and imaginary
part converge, and are

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:03.780 align:middle line:84%
bounded if and only if the
real and imaginary part, which

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:06.960 align:middle line:84%
are sequences of real
numbers, are bounded.

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:09.690 align:middle line:84%
So Bolzano-Weierstrass
immediately implies

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:12.180 align:middle line:84%
a similar statement
for complex numbers,

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.089 align:middle line:84%
that every bounded
sequence of complex numbers

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:18.470 align:middle line:90%
has a convergent subsequence.

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:19.010 align:middle line:90%
so.

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:20.240 align:middle line:90%
How would we use that?

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:24.577 align:middle line:84%
What we're going to do is
we're going to expand uN's.

00:17:24.577 --> 00:17:26.119 align:middle line:84%
And this is where
we'll use that this

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:29.850 align:middle line:84%
as an orthonormal basis in
terms of this orthonormal basis.

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:33.290 align:middle line:84%
So for each K, we'll
have a sequence

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:38.240 align:middle line:84%
of complex numbers given by
the entry, the K-th entry,

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.495 align:middle line:84%
of course the K-th
entry of this sequence.

00:17:41.495 --> 00:17:44.060 align:middle line:90%


00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:47.510 align:middle line:84%
And that will be a bounded
sequence of complex numbers

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:50.600 align:middle line:84%
because we're
assuming K is bounded,

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:53.570 align:middle line:84%
and therefore this
sequence is bounded in K.

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:56.510 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going to end
up having is entry by entry,

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:59.900 align:middle line:84%
a bounded sequence
of complex numbers

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:04.220 align:middle line:84%
where these complex numbers
represent the K-th entry

00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:08.940 align:middle line:90%
of the sequence of vectors.

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:11.790 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to then
take a subsequence that

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:13.962 align:middle line:90%
converges for the first entry.

00:18:13.962 --> 00:18:16.170 align:middle line:84%
We're going to take a
subsequence of that subsequence

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:19.410 align:middle line:84%
to get a subsequence
which converges

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:22.710 align:middle line:84%
for the first and second entry,
and then so on and so on,

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.230 align:middle line:90%
a diagonalization argument.

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:29.740 align:middle line:84%
And that subsequence
we'll show converges.

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:33.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's not that simple
because if you can do something

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:37.710 align:middle line:84%
10 times it doesn't mean you
can get some uniform control.

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:42.210 align:middle line:84%
We just have control for any
finite number of entries.

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:46.710 align:middle line:84%
What saves us in the end is
this equi-small tail condition,

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:53.160 align:middle line:84%
which allows us to
basically throw away

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:54.790 align:middle line:90%
infinitely many entries.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:58.210 align:middle line:90%


00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:01.120 align:middle line:84%
That's what this says is that
you can always choose an N,

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:05.120 align:middle line:84%
so that the tail end of
the entries don't matter.

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:10.360 align:middle line:84%
And so that, with having control
over the first large block

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:13.660 align:middle line:84%
of entries, will be
enough to conclude.

00:19:13.660 --> 00:19:15.130 align:middle line:90%
So that was the roadmap.

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:16.910 align:middle line:84%
If you didn't follow
the roadmap, fine.

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:18.202 align:middle line:90%
Hopefully you follow the proof.

00:19:18.202 --> 00:19:23.290 align:middle line:90%


00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:26.200 align:middle line:90%
But it's a diagonal argument.

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:33.280 align:middle line:84%
And those are relatively
easy to understand,

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:38.110 align:middle line:84%
somewhat difficult to write
down, so I will do my best.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:52.000 align:middle line:84%
So since K is
bounded, that implies

00:19:52.000 --> 00:20:00.520 align:middle line:84%
there exists a c non-negative
such that for all n,

00:20:00.520 --> 00:20:04.930 align:middle line:84%
norm of u sub n is less
than or equal to C.

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:06.790 align:middle line:90%
The C is independent of n.

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:11.970 align:middle line:90%
It just depends on the set K.

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:18.060 align:middle line:84%
And therefore that
sets for all K and n--

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:27.560 align:middle line:84%
I should say for all K, for
all n, if I look at the K-th--

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:30.650 align:middle line:84%
if you let me call this
the Fourier coefficient

00:20:30.650 --> 00:20:35.870 align:middle line:84%
in this basis in this
orthonormal basis of ek,

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:39.320 align:middle line:84%
if I look at the K-th
Fourier coefficient,

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:46.510 align:middle line:84%
this is bounded by
Cauchy-Schwartz, u n ek.

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:48.920 align:middle line:84%
And these are orthonormal
so this has unit length.

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:51.220 align:middle line:90%
This is bounded by c.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:55.300 align:middle line:84%
So for each fixed K,
this is a sequence

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:57.235 align:middle line:84%
in N of complex numbers
which is bounded.

00:20:57.235 --> 00:21:04.380 align:middle line:90%


00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:09.370 align:middle line:84%
The sequence consisting of
the K-th Fourier coefficient

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:17.830 align:middle line:84%
of the elements u sub n, so n
is the thing that's changing,

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:25.455 align:middle line:84%
is the bounded sequence
of complex numbers.

00:21:25.455 --> 00:21:28.520 align:middle line:90%


00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:32.960 align:middle line:84%
So now this is where we're going
to start choosing subsequences

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:36.080 align:middle line:84%
of subsequences so that we get
convergence along the entries.

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:39.470 align:middle line:90%


00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:53.420 align:middle line:84%
So since u sub is bounded,
this implies that there exists

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:56.390 align:middle line:84%
by Bolzano-Weierstrass,
a subsequence,

00:21:56.390 --> 00:21:59.960 align:middle line:84%
which I'll denote u
sub n sub 1 of k--

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:03.170 align:middle line:90%


00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:04.190 align:middle line:90%
let me not use k.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:07.520 align:middle line:84%
Let's use-- what
do I use here? j--

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.560 align:middle line:90%


00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:12.795 align:middle line:90%
e1, so 1, 1--

00:22:12.795 --> 00:22:20.740 align:middle line:90%


00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:34.980 align:middle line:90%
in n, which converges in c.

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:48.520 align:middle line:90%


00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:54.500 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I now have a subsequence
of the original sequence

00:22:54.500 --> 00:23:02.960 align:middle line:84%
of the un's so that the
first entry converges here.

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:05.220 align:middle line:90%
Or this sequence converges.

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:06.410 align:middle line:90%
Now I'm going to take--

00:23:06.410 --> 00:23:21.500 align:middle line:84%
OK, so since un1 of j e2, this
is still a bounded sequence

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:30.850 align:middle line:84%
by what I showed here,
bounded sequence.

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:36.050 align:middle line:84%
This implies there
exists a subsequence.

00:23:36.050 --> 00:23:49.270 align:middle line:84%
Now I'll call this
subsequence un2 of j e2 j.

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:52.060 align:middle line:84%
So in 2 of j, this is
a sequence of integers.

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:54.460 align:middle line:84%
That is a subset of
these guys that's

00:23:54.460 --> 00:24:06.870 align:middle line:90%
increasing --of un1 of j.

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:07.755 align:middle line:90%
So I should say--

00:24:07.755 --> 00:24:15.730 align:middle line:90%


00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:19.290 align:middle line:90%
which converges.

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:26.290 align:middle line:84%
So now note, this
sequence converges.

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:37.520 align:middle line:84%
But the n2 of j, so this is
a subsequence of integers

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.040 align:middle line:90%
of the n1 of j's.

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:44.860 align:middle line:90%


00:24:44.860 --> 00:24:47.800 align:middle line:84%
Since the n2 of
j's a subsequence

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:57.640 align:middle line:84%
of the n1 of j's,
we get that limit j

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:02.920 align:middle line:90%
goes to infinity of un1 of j--

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:05.215 align:middle line:90%
or should say n2 of j--

00:25:05.215 --> 00:25:06.550 align:middle line:90%
e1.

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:12.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is a subsequence
of this sequence.

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:16.910 align:middle line:84%
So the n2 of j is just a
subsequence of n1 of j's.

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:21.800 align:middle line:84%
So since this sequence
in j converges,

00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:23.900 align:middle line:90%
this subsequence also converges.

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:26.730 align:middle line:90%


00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:35.300 align:middle line:84%
But now we've chosen the two
so that we also have not just

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:37.280 align:middle line:84%
convergence along
the first entry,

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:42.725 align:middle line:84%
but convergence along
the second entry.

00:25:42.725 --> 00:25:46.670 align:middle line:90%


00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:48.260 align:middle line:90%
But now you see what--

00:25:48.260 --> 00:25:51.800 align:middle line:84%
so this is how the
argument goes, that now I

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:54.500 align:middle line:84%
will take a subsequence
of the n2 of j

00:25:54.500 --> 00:25:58.760 align:middle line:84%
so that the
subsequence converges

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:01.940 align:middle line:90%
when I pair it with e3.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:05.450 align:middle line:84%
And then I take a subsequence
of that subsequence

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:07.560 align:middle line:84%
so that I get e4 and
so on, and so on.

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:12.020 align:middle line:84%
And all of these subsequences
are subsequences of--

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:13.670 align:middle line:84%
or these subsequences
of integers

00:26:13.670 --> 00:26:16.080 align:middle line:84%
are subsequences of the
previous set of integers,

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:20.105 align:middle line:84%
which are again a subsequence
of the original integers n.

00:26:20.105 --> 00:26:31.370 align:middle line:90%


00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:40.385 align:middle line:90%
So then what do we get?

00:26:40.385 --> 00:26:43.130 align:middle line:90%


00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:52.370 align:middle line:84%
For all l there exists a
subsequence of integers

00:26:52.370 --> 00:27:00.310 align:middle line:84%
in l of j of the previous
set of integers in,

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:17.280 align:middle line:84%
let's say, l minus 1 of j, such
that for all k between 1 and l

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:30.595 align:middle line:84%
limit as j goes to infinity
of u in l of j ek exists.

00:27:30.595 --> 00:27:34.880 align:middle line:90%


00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:38.450 align:middle line:90%
So I hope this is clear.

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:41.190 align:middle line:84%
So you just take a subsequence
of the original sequence

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:42.800 align:middle line:84%
so that the first
entry converges,

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:45.020 align:middle line:84%
take a subsequence of that
so that the second entry

00:27:45.020 --> 00:27:46.920 align:middle line:90%
converges, and so on and so on.

00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:54.260 align:middle line:84%
So then you have this
nested subsequences so

00:27:54.260 --> 00:28:00.115 align:middle line:84%
that at each fixed l, you have
convergence of the first l

00:28:00.115 --> 00:28:00.615 align:middle line:90%
entries.

00:28:00.615 --> 00:28:03.470 align:middle line:90%


00:28:03.470 --> 00:28:05.850 align:middle line:84%
And so what you now
do is that you pick--

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:18.600 align:middle line:84%
so let me write or pick
vl to be the diagonal

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:21.090 align:middle line:90%
along this sequence.

00:28:21.090 --> 00:28:35.460 align:middle line:84%
So u in l of l, where l
equals 1, 2, 3, and so on.

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:38.140 align:middle line:90%


00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:46.390 align:middle line:84%
Then what we get is since the
l-th subsequence converges--

00:28:46.390 --> 00:28:52.330 align:middle line:90%
or the k-th entry for one--

00:28:52.330 --> 00:28:56.740 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let me back up and
say this one more time.

00:28:56.740 --> 00:29:02.680 align:middle line:84%
Since four fixed l here I have
convergence of the first l

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:05.620 align:middle line:84%
entries, by picking
along the diagonal

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:30.090 align:middle line:84%
I get a subsequence of
un such that for all k

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:38.780 align:middle line:84%
I have convergence for the k-th
entry as l goes to infinity,

00:29:38.780 --> 00:29:39.780 align:middle line:90%
converges.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:46.150 align:middle line:90%


00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:50.940 align:middle line:84%
All right, so I can take
subsequences of subsequences

00:29:50.940 --> 00:29:52.770 align:middle line:84%
and obtain a
subsequence in the end

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:57.600 align:middle line:84%
so that for this subsequence
of the original sequence un's,

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:02.100 align:middle line:84%
I have convergence of the
entries entry by entry,

00:30:02.100 --> 00:30:04.830 align:middle line:84%
or I have convergence of
the Fourier coefficients

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:08.410 align:middle line:90%
as l goes to infinity.

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:12.510 align:middle line:84%
And now we're going to use
this equi-small tails to prove

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:16.245 align:middle line:84%
that the sequence
v sub l converges.

00:30:16.245 --> 00:30:18.120 align:middle line:84%
We're just actually
going to show its Cauchy.

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:20.010 align:middle line:84%
Since H is a Hilbert
space, we then

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:25.420 align:middle line:90%
conclude that it must converge.

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:36.020 align:middle line:90%
So claim vl is Cauchy.

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:38.300 align:middle line:84%
And then that will conclude
the proof because then it

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:42.350 align:middle line:84%
must converge in H. And by
what I said at the beginning,

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:45.530 align:middle line:84%
since this is a
sequence of elements

00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:48.920 align:middle line:84%
in k, which is a closed set,
the limit has to be in k.

00:30:48.920 --> 00:30:51.080 align:middle line:84%
All right, so we just
need to show it's Cauchy,

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:52.500 align:middle line:90%
and then the proof is done.

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:54.410 align:middle line:90%
So let epsilon be positive.

00:30:54.410 --> 00:30:59.470 align:middle line:90%


00:30:59.470 --> 00:31:13.220 align:middle line:84%
So since k has
equi-small tails--

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:15.880 align:middle line:90%
let's go over here--

00:31:15.880 --> 00:31:21.930 align:middle line:84%
there exists natural
number capital N

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:31.250 align:middle line:84%
so that for all l we have
that the sum of squares k

00:31:31.250 --> 00:31:38.330 align:middle line:84%
bigger than N of the
tails, vl, ek squared,

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:41.270 align:middle line:84%
is less than epsilon
squared over 16.

00:31:41.270 --> 00:31:42.260 align:middle line:90%
Why 16?

00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:48.410 align:middle line:84%
Because I want everything to
come out correct in the end.

00:31:48.410 --> 00:32:01.870 align:middle line:84%
So since the N sequences
given by the first N

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:10.690 align:middle line:84%
entries of the sequence vl, so
since the N sequences vl, e1 l

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:15.970 align:middle line:90%
up to vl, vn, l--

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:17.060 align:middle line:90%
so these are only--

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:22.030 align:middle line:84%
so this is the bit where I
said equi-small tails allows

00:32:22.030 --> 00:32:28.840 align:middle line:84%
us to throw away the tail
end of these entries,

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:33.130 align:middle line:84%
and just control over the first
finitely many entries gives us

00:32:33.130 --> 00:32:36.320 align:middle line:84%
the condition or the
convergence that we want.

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:40.010 align:middle line:90%
This is where that's happening.

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:50.210 align:middle line:84%
So since these converge, there
exists a natural number M

00:32:50.210 --> 00:32:58.950 align:middle line:84%
so that for all l, m
bigger than capital M,

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:07.170 align:middle line:84%
I have that the sum n equals
1 to capital N of vl, ek

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:18.760 align:middle line:84%
minus vm, ek squared is less
than epsilon squared over 4.

00:33:18.760 --> 00:33:24.370 align:middle line:84%
So each of these are
convergent sequences.

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:27.130 align:middle line:84%
So each of these are
Cauchy sequences.

00:33:27.130 --> 00:33:31.270 align:middle line:84%
So for each one, I can
find a capital M sub 1

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:36.480 align:middle line:84%
so that just one of these
entries is very small for l

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:38.820 align:middle line:84%
and m bigger than
that capital M sub 1.

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:42.540 align:middle line:84%
And then I can do the
next one so that for--

00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:44.490 align:middle line:90%
so this should be k.

00:33:44.490 --> 00:33:47.300 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what that
looks like, but k.

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:49.940 align:middle line:84%
So that for k equals 2, it's
very small, and then up to k

00:33:49.940 --> 00:33:50.600 align:middle line:90%
equals n.

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:58.060 align:middle line:84%
And then I choose capital M to
be the maximum over all those M

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:00.550 align:middle line:90%
sub N's.

00:34:00.550 --> 00:34:03.430 align:middle line:84%
So I can always choose this
because, again, there's

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:07.879 align:middle line:84%
only finitely many conditions
I have to verify, all right.

00:34:07.879 --> 00:34:14.239 align:middle line:90%


00:34:14.239 --> 00:34:20.510 align:middle line:84%
So now I claim this
capital M works.

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:24.380 align:middle line:84%
Then for all lm bigger
than or equal to M,

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:30.199 align:middle line:84%
if we look at the
norm of vl minus vm,

00:34:30.199 --> 00:34:39.080 align:middle line:90%
so this is equal to sum--

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:46.670 align:middle line:84%
it should say k equals
1 to n of vl minus vm.

00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:51.469 align:middle line:90%


00:34:51.469 --> 00:34:58.430 align:middle line:90%
So by k equals--

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:07.700 align:middle line:84%
k bigger than N, vl
minus vm, ek 1/2.

00:35:07.700 --> 00:35:13.370 align:middle line:84%
So here I'm using the fact that
we have an orthonormal basis.

00:35:13.370 --> 00:35:18.230 align:middle line:84%
Then in that case, the norm is
equal to the sum of the squares

00:35:18.230 --> 00:35:21.540 align:middle line:90%
of the Fourier coefficients.

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:23.630 align:middle line:84%
Bessel's inequality would
say this is less than

00:35:23.630 --> 00:35:24.770 align:middle line:90%
or equal to this side.

00:35:24.770 --> 00:35:27.380 align:middle line:84%
It wouldn't give us any control
we want over the actual norm.

00:35:27.380 --> 00:35:30.080 align:middle line:84%
But because we're working
in orthonormal basis,

00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:32.930 align:middle line:84%
the norm of that is equal
to the sum of squares,

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:33.980 align:middle line:90%
take the square root.

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:39.760 align:middle line:90%


00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:43.290 align:middle line:84%
And so now the square
root of A plus B,

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:46.030 align:middle line:84%
that's always less than or equal
to the square root of A plus

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:53.020 align:middle line:84%
the square root of B. So
that's less than A equals 1

00:35:53.020 --> 00:36:15.880 align:middle line:84%
to N of vl minus vm squared 1/2
plus k bigger than n vl, ek.

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:21.280 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just going to write this
out a little differently, vm,

00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:26.170 align:middle line:90%
ek, 1/2.

00:36:26.170 --> 00:36:28.990 align:middle line:84%
Now this part we
know is less than--

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:32.260 align:middle line:84%
by how we've chosen capital
M, this finite part is small.

00:36:32.260 --> 00:36:34.840 align:middle line:90%
It's less than epsilon over 2.

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:39.870 align:middle line:90%


00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:44.070 align:middle line:84%
So that's less than
epsilon over 2.

00:36:44.070 --> 00:36:50.250 align:middle line:84%
Plus now we use the triangle
inequality now for little l2.

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:55.530 align:middle line:84%
I can think of this as
the sum in little l2--

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:57.960 align:middle line:84%
or I can think of
this as the norm

00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:03.960 align:middle line:84%
in little l2 of this sequence
in k minus this sequence in k.

00:37:03.960 --> 00:37:14.470 align:middle line:84%
So this is less
than or equal to k

00:37:14.470 --> 00:37:43.430 align:middle line:84%
bigger than N, vl, ek, 1/2 plus
sum k bigger than vm, ek 1/2

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:44.790 align:middle line:90%
All right, we're almost done.

00:37:44.790 --> 00:37:48.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than,
the square part is less

00:37:48.290 --> 00:37:50.700 align:middle line:90%
than epsilon squared over 16.

00:37:50.700 --> 00:37:56.530 align:middle line:84%
So taking the square root of
that, I get epsilon over 4

00:37:56.530 --> 00:38:00.910 align:middle line:84%
by how we chose N. So this is
based on how we chose N, plus--

00:38:00.910 --> 00:38:02.740 align:middle line:84%
which came from the
equi-small tails part.

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:08.680 align:middle line:84%
And this part also because of
N is less than epsilon over 4

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:09.680 align:middle line:90%
equals epsilon.

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:13.450 align:middle line:84%
So we've now shown the claim
that the subsequence v sub l

00:38:13.450 --> 00:38:16.735 align:middle line:84%
is Cauchy, and therefore it
converges since H is complete.

00:38:16.735 --> 00:38:25.080 align:middle line:90%


00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:34.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, so for example, one
can readily verify now

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:35.725 align:middle line:90%
that the following subset.

00:38:35.725 --> 00:38:38.650 align:middle line:90%


00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:45.790 align:middle line:84%
Let k be the i set of
all sequences n little l2

00:38:45.790 --> 00:38:52.120 align:middle line:84%
with the property that ak
is less than or equal to 2

00:38:52.120 --> 00:38:53.170 align:middle line:90%
to the minus k.

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:55.495 align:middle line:84%
So this is not a
subspace, it's a subset--

00:38:55.495 --> 00:39:01.980 align:middle line:90%


00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:03.960 align:middle line:90%
is compact.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:06.540 align:middle line:84%
This subset is known
as the Hilbert cube.

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:10.970 align:middle line:90%


00:39:10.970 --> 00:39:15.570 align:middle line:84%
All right, now maybe
it's a bit unwieldy

00:39:15.570 --> 00:39:20.430 align:middle line:84%
that our condition
for being compact

00:39:20.430 --> 00:39:25.380 align:middle line:84%
is phrased in terms of
an orthonormal basis.

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:28.350 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that's not
so simple to verify

00:39:28.350 --> 00:39:36.190 align:middle line:84%
or definitely not
necessarily, what's

00:39:36.190 --> 00:39:38.470 align:middle line:84%
the word I'm looking
for, canonical--

00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:40.830 align:middle line:84%
I guess that's the
word I'm looking for--

00:39:40.830 --> 00:39:42.580 align:middle line:84%
in the sense that we
have to make a choice

00:39:42.580 --> 00:39:43.750 align:middle line:90%
to verify compactness.

00:39:43.750 --> 00:39:45.910 align:middle line:84%
But a different way
of characterizing

00:39:45.910 --> 00:39:48.500 align:middle line:84%
compact subsets of a Hilbert
space is the following.

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:50.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, I'm not going to
give the proof of this.

00:39:50.960 --> 00:39:54.190 align:middle line:84%
You can look it up
in Melrose's notes.

00:39:54.190 --> 00:39:56.380 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll say a word
about why you should

00:39:56.380 --> 00:39:58.960 align:middle line:90%
believe or think it's true.

00:39:58.960 --> 00:40:02.570 align:middle line:84%
But, again, it involves
a diagonal argument,

00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:05.590 align:middle line:84%
which that was tough
enough to do here,

00:40:05.590 --> 00:40:08.122 align:middle line:84%
or at least painful
enough to write out.

00:40:08.122 --> 00:40:09.580 align:middle line:84%
And so I don't want
to do it again.

00:40:09.580 --> 00:40:11.770 align:middle line:90%
So we have the following that--

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:18.400 align:middle line:84%
and this theorem also holds in
a nonseparable Hilbert space,

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:22.450 align:middle line:84%
basically by a trick of reducing
it to a separable Hilbert

00:40:22.450 --> 00:40:24.070 align:middle line:90%
space, but that's OK.

00:40:24.070 --> 00:40:33.020 align:middle line:84%
A subset K of a Hilbert space
H is compact if and only

00:40:33.020 --> 00:40:39.060 align:middle line:90%
if K is closed, bounded.

00:40:39.060 --> 00:40:41.610 align:middle line:90%


00:40:41.610 --> 00:40:43.020 align:middle line:84%
And the following
condition holds

00:40:43.020 --> 00:40:46.860 align:middle line:84%
what you can think of
as K can be approximated

00:40:46.860 --> 00:40:50.350 align:middle line:90%
by finite dimensional subspaces.

00:40:50.350 --> 00:40:53.530 align:middle line:90%


00:40:53.530 --> 00:40:58.670 align:middle line:84%
And for all epsilon
positive, there

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:16.170 align:middle line:84%
exists a finite dimensional
subspace W in K such

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:24.540 align:middle line:84%
that for all u in K, if I
look at the distance from u

00:41:24.540 --> 00:41:27.953 align:middle line:84%
to this finite dimensional
subspace-- which

00:41:27.953 --> 00:41:29.370 align:middle line:84%
I think I said at
one point, maybe

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:31.770 align:middle line:84%
I didn't, but a finite
dimensional subspace is always

00:41:31.770 --> 00:41:39.768 align:middle line:90%
a closed subspace in H.

00:41:39.768 --> 00:41:42.000 align:middle line:84%
A finite dimensional
subspace is always closed.

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:46.970 align:middle line:84%
But if I look at the
distance from this point

00:41:46.970 --> 00:41:52.020 align:middle line:84%
to the subspace, this
finite dimensional subspace,

00:41:52.020 --> 00:41:55.150 align:middle line:90%
this is less than epsilon.

00:41:55.150 --> 00:41:59.040 align:middle line:84%
So a subset in the Hilbert
space is compact if and only

00:41:59.040 --> 00:42:00.880 align:middle line:84%
if it's closed,
bounded, and if you like

00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:04.500 align:middle line:84%
can be approximated
by compact subsets

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:11.130 align:middle line:84%
of finite dimensional subspaces,
another way to think about it.

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:15.240 align:middle line:84%
Now, why is this believable
in the first place?

00:42:15.240 --> 00:42:17.720 align:middle line:90%
Well, what this-- where is it?

00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:20.950 align:middle line:90%


00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:29.070 align:middle line:84%
What the equi-small tail
condition says is basically

00:42:29.070 --> 00:42:36.270 align:middle line:84%
that all elements in K
can be approximated by--

00:42:36.270 --> 00:42:39.000 align:middle line:84%
if you let epsilon
be positive-- can

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:42.630 align:middle line:84%
be approximated by the
subspace consisting

00:42:42.630 --> 00:42:49.260 align:middle line:84%
of the span of the first
one up to N basis vectors

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:51.360 align:middle line:90%
or orthonormal basis vectors.

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:52.620 align:middle line:90%
That's what this is.

00:42:52.620 --> 00:42:54.760 align:middle line:84%
For all epsilon the
tails are small.

00:42:54.760 --> 00:42:58.770 align:middle line:84%
Another way of saying that
is that for all epsilon,

00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:00.870 align:middle line:84%
you can approximate
any element in the set

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:07.510 align:middle line:84%
K in the span of the
first N basis vectors.

00:43:07.510 --> 00:43:18.980 align:middle line:84%
So K has to be close to
finite dimensional subspace.

00:43:18.980 --> 00:43:22.020 align:middle line:84%
But one can go the
opposite direction as well,

00:43:22.020 --> 00:43:28.010 align:middle line:84%
showing that finite
dimensional subspace--

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:30.350 align:middle line:84%
being able to be
approximated by those guys

00:43:30.350 --> 00:43:31.640 align:middle line:90%
implies that K is compact.

00:43:31.640 --> 00:43:34.010 align:middle line:84%
And it's another diagonal
argument that I just

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:36.080 align:middle line:84%
don't want to write out
and rather just start

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:39.802 align:middle line:84%
using these conditions
instead to start saying things

00:43:39.802 --> 00:43:41.510 align:middle line:84%
about when we can
solve certain equations

00:43:41.510 --> 00:43:43.520 align:middle line:84%
and certain
interesting operators

00:43:43.520 --> 00:43:47.450 align:middle line:90%
that arise quite naturally.

00:43:47.450 --> 00:43:50.400 align:middle line:90%


00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:57.860 align:middle line:84%
So let's start talking about
certain classes of operators.

00:43:57.860 --> 00:43:59.540 align:middle line:84%
And we should start
with the simplest,

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:05.110 align:middle line:90%
which are finite rank operator.

00:44:05.110 --> 00:44:12.450 align:middle line:84%
So the only linear operators
you came into this class knowing

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:13.950 align:middle line:90%
probably--

00:44:13.950 --> 00:44:17.760 align:middle line:84%
if you didn't and you were more
sophisticated, that's good--

00:44:17.760 --> 00:44:22.830 align:middle line:90%
but were matrices.

00:44:22.830 --> 00:44:26.340 align:middle line:84%
Now a matrix is just a
linear transformation

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:29.510 align:middle line:84%
defined in terms of an
orthonormal-- or not

00:44:29.510 --> 00:44:35.597 align:middle line:84%
orthonormal basis, but a basis
in both the domain and target.

00:44:35.597 --> 00:44:37.180 align:middle line:84%
We picked the target
to be the domain.

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:38.680 align:middle line:84%
We just fix a basis,
and then we can

00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:43.950 align:middle line:84%
express a linear transformation
as an array of numbers.

00:44:43.950 --> 00:44:48.390 align:middle line:84%
Finite rank operators
are the generalization

00:44:48.390 --> 00:44:54.780 align:middle line:84%
of what you think of matrices
now for to Hilbert spaces.

00:44:54.780 --> 00:44:59.730 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see that
in just a minute.

00:44:59.730 --> 00:45:07.490 align:middle line:84%
And what follows H
is a Hilbert space.

00:45:07.490 --> 00:45:10.270 align:middle line:90%


00:45:10.270 --> 00:45:17.790 align:middle line:84%
And instead of writing the space
of bounded linear operators,

00:45:17.790 --> 00:45:23.460 align:middle line:84%
B H, H, I'm just going to
concatenate this by dropping

00:45:23.460 --> 00:45:24.390 align:middle line:90%
one of those H's.

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:27.240 align:middle line:84%
So the space of bounded linear
operators from H to itself

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:36.200 align:middle line:90%
will be denoted by B of H.

00:45:36.200 --> 00:45:40.790 align:middle line:84%
So finite rank
operators, these are,

00:45:40.790 --> 00:45:43.205 align:middle line:84%
like I said, these will
be the analog of matrices.

00:45:43.205 --> 00:45:45.920 align:middle line:90%


00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:54.470 align:middle line:84%
But let me give a
invariant definition first.

00:45:54.470 --> 00:46:06.030 align:middle line:84%
So a bounded linear operator
is a finite rank operator

00:46:06.030 --> 00:46:27.260 align:middle line:84%
if the range of T which
is a subspace of H

00:46:27.260 --> 00:46:32.570 align:middle line:90%
is finite dimensional, OK.

00:46:32.570 --> 00:46:37.680 align:middle line:90%


00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:42.970 align:middle line:84%
So, for example, let me
give you the simple--

00:46:42.970 --> 00:46:48.630 align:middle line:84%
well of course the zero
operator is a simple one.

00:46:48.630 --> 00:46:52.350 align:middle line:84%
If H is a finite dimensional
Hilbert space, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:55.290 align:middle line:84%
then every bounded
linear operator

00:46:55.290 --> 00:46:57.930 align:middle line:84%
is a finite rank operator
because the range is always

00:46:57.930 --> 00:47:02.520 align:middle line:84%
contained in Cn, which
is finite dimensional.

00:47:02.520 --> 00:47:06.720 align:middle line:84%
For example on little
l2, if I define

00:47:06.720 --> 00:47:20.100 align:middle line:84%
Ta to be now the sequence
given by a1 over 1,

00:47:20.100 --> 00:47:30.990 align:middle line:84%
a2 over 2, up to an over n for
some n and then 0 afterwards,

00:47:30.990 --> 00:47:39.530 align:middle line:84%
and this is for a equals
sequence in little l2,

00:47:39.530 --> 00:47:50.520 align:middle line:84%
then T is a finite rank
operator because the range

00:47:50.520 --> 00:47:56.280 align:middle line:84%
of this operator-- so
here N is a fixed number

00:47:56.280 --> 00:47:57.480 align:middle line:90%
acting on sequences.

00:47:57.480 --> 00:47:59.370 align:middle line:84%
And little l2 just
spits out the first--

00:47:59.370 --> 00:48:02.760 align:middle line:90%


00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:04.890 align:middle line:84%
let's make this
even more explicit.

00:48:04.890 --> 00:48:07.680 align:middle line:90%
Let's say that's 5.

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:09.720 align:middle line:84%
This is a finite rank
operator because it's

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:11.580 align:middle line:84%
contained in the
subspace consisting

00:48:11.580 --> 00:48:17.740 align:middle line:84%
of those sequences which
are 0 after the fifth entry.

00:48:17.740 --> 00:48:18.990 align:middle line:90%
And that's finite dimensional.

00:48:18.990 --> 00:48:22.860 align:middle line:84%
A basis is given by the sequence
consisting of 0's with a 1

00:48:22.860 --> 00:48:26.980 align:middle line:84%
here, 0's with a 1 here, 0's
with a 1 here, and so on.

00:48:26.980 --> 00:48:28.650 align:middle line:84%
So this is a finite
rank operator.

00:48:28.650 --> 00:48:32.300 align:middle line:90%


00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:37.550 align:middle line:84%
And so let me just put over here
that as far as notation goes,

00:48:37.550 --> 00:48:45.865 align:middle line:84%
we write T is in RH, R
for rank, finite rank.

00:48:45.865 --> 00:48:49.860 align:middle line:90%


00:48:49.860 --> 00:48:52.500 align:middle line:84%
All right, so R of
H, this is the set

00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:53.910 align:middle line:90%
of all finite rank operators.

00:48:53.910 --> 00:48:55.005 align:middle line:90%
Now, it's not just a set.

00:48:55.005 --> 00:49:07.190 align:middle line:90%


00:49:07.190 --> 00:49:14.270 align:middle line:84%
It's easy to see that this is
a subspace of the Banach space

00:49:14.270 --> 00:49:20.190 align:middle line:84%
consisting of all bounded linear
operators from H to itself.

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:21.180 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:49:21.180 --> 00:49:25.400 align:middle line:84%
Well, so if I take
an operator that

00:49:25.400 --> 00:49:29.000 align:middle line:84%
has finite dimensional range
and multiply it by a scalar,

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:32.930 align:middle line:84%
then the range of the scalar
multiple of that operator

00:49:32.930 --> 00:49:36.110 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the range
of the original operator

00:49:36.110 --> 00:49:38.150 align:middle line:90%
unless it's the scalar 0.

00:49:38.150 --> 00:49:40.340 align:middle line:84%
So that will be
finite dimensional.

00:49:40.340 --> 00:49:43.860 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if I take
two finite rank operators

00:49:43.860 --> 00:49:48.050 align:middle line:84%
and I consider their
sum, the range of the sum

00:49:48.050 --> 00:49:55.520 align:middle line:84%
is going to be contained in
the direct sum of the ranges.

00:49:55.520 --> 00:50:01.520 align:middle line:84%
And the direct sum of two finite
dimensional spaces, subspaces,

00:50:01.520 --> 00:50:04.200 align:middle line:90%
is again finite dimensional.

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:08.120 align:middle line:84%
So if one has dimension 5 and
the other has dimension 6,

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:12.920 align:middle line:84%
then the direct sum of these
two will be of dimension 11.

00:50:12.920 --> 00:50:16.720 align:middle line:84%
So that is me talking
you through the proof

00:50:16.720 --> 00:50:18.020 align:middle line:90%
that this is a subspace.

00:50:18.020 --> 00:50:23.150 align:middle line:84%
So take a minute
to write it down.

00:50:23.150 --> 00:50:35.920 align:middle line:84%
But now let me prove that these
finite rank operators really

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:39.625 align:middle line:90%
are essentially matrices.

00:50:39.625 --> 00:50:50.740 align:middle line:90%


00:50:50.740 --> 00:50:53.590 align:middle line:84%
I have the following theorem
characterizing finite rank

00:50:53.590 --> 00:50:54.700 align:middle line:90%
operators.

00:50:54.700 --> 00:50:59.890 align:middle line:84%
So T is a finite rank
operator if and only

00:50:59.890 --> 00:51:13.502 align:middle line:84%
if there exists an orthonormal,
finite orthonormal subset ek,

00:51:13.502 --> 00:51:25.590 align:middle line:84%
k equals 1 to some integer
L. And complex numbers

00:51:25.590 --> 00:51:30.920 align:middle line:90%
cij ij equals 1 to l.

00:51:30.920 --> 00:51:39.420 align:middle line:84%
So this is subsequence-- or
a subset of complex numbers

00:51:39.420 --> 00:51:44.490 align:middle line:84%
such that if I want to
compute what T applied to u

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:56.010 align:middle line:84%
is, it's just going to be sum
i equals j, ij goes from 1 to l

00:51:56.010 --> 00:52:07.290 align:middle line:84%
of these numbers Cij times
the j entry of u times ei.

00:52:07.290 --> 00:52:12.270 align:middle line:84%
So, in other words, T
corresponds to a matrix where

00:52:12.270 --> 00:52:15.420 align:middle line:84%
the coefficients are
these c's, basically,

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:19.935 align:middle line:84%
acting on this finite
dimensional subspace of ek's.

00:52:19.935 --> 00:52:28.390 align:middle line:90%


00:52:28.390 --> 00:52:31.340 align:middle line:90%
So let's give the proof of this.

00:52:31.340 --> 00:52:34.580 align:middle line:90%
One direction is immediate.

00:52:34.580 --> 00:52:38.390 align:middle line:84%
If T has such a
representation, then

00:52:38.390 --> 00:52:41.420 align:middle line:84%
it has finite rank
because then the image is

00:52:41.420 --> 00:52:45.470 align:middle line:84%
contained in the span of the
ei's for i going from 1 to l.

00:52:45.470 --> 00:52:47.820 align:middle line:84%
And that's a finite
dimensional subspace.

00:52:47.820 --> 00:52:54.460 align:middle line:84%
So this is clear that this
condition implies that T

00:52:54.460 --> 00:52:55.850 align:middle line:90%
is a finite rank operator.

00:52:55.850 --> 00:53:02.680 align:middle line:84%
So let's go the
opposite direction.

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:16.880 align:middle line:84%
So since the range of T
is finite dimensional, we

00:53:16.880 --> 00:53:30.800 align:middle line:84%
can find an orthonormal
basis, call it ek,

00:53:30.800 --> 00:53:42.540 align:middle line:84%
k equals 1 to N such that T
applied to u is equal to--

00:53:42.540 --> 00:53:46.440 align:middle line:84%
so it's an element
in the span of--

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:49.500 align:middle line:90%
so this spans the range.

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:56.060 align:middle line:84%
So I can write this as some
numbers times the ek's.

00:53:56.060 --> 00:53:58.575 align:middle line:90%


00:53:58.575 --> 00:53:59.700 align:middle line:90%
And what are these numbers?

00:53:59.700 --> 00:54:01.742 align:middle line:84%
Well, these numbers, since
these are orthonormal,

00:54:01.742 --> 00:54:06.100 align:middle line:84%
have to be T applied
to u in a product ek.

00:54:06.100 --> 00:54:09.460 align:middle line:90%


00:54:09.460 --> 00:54:11.095 align:middle line:90%
All right, so Tu--

00:54:11.095 --> 00:54:13.660 align:middle line:90%


00:54:13.660 --> 00:54:17.590 align:middle line:84%
so the ranges spanned by
these orthonormal vectors,

00:54:17.590 --> 00:54:21.040 align:middle line:84%
and anything in the span
of this orthonormal vectors

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:25.205 align:middle line:84%
has to be written as that
thing in a product ek times ek.

00:54:25.205 --> 00:54:27.940 align:middle line:90%


00:54:27.940 --> 00:54:30.250 align:middle line:84%
But let me now
make an observation

00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:39.710 align:middle line:84%
that this thing
right here we can

00:54:39.710 --> 00:54:53.880 align:middle line:84%
write as using the adjoint as
u in a product T star ek, ek.

00:54:53.880 --> 00:54:56.490 align:middle line:90%


00:54:56.490 --> 00:55:00.990 align:middle line:84%
The adjoint operator,
so T star which

00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:03.870 align:middle line:84%
goes from H to H, remember
satisfies this property

00:55:03.870 --> 00:55:07.200 align:middle line:90%
that given any two vectors a--

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:10.320 align:middle line:84%
or u and v, T applied
to u in a product v

00:55:10.320 --> 00:55:13.440 align:middle line:84%
is equal to u in a
product T star v. That's

00:55:13.440 --> 00:55:17.580 align:middle line:84%
the defining property
of the adjoint

00:55:17.580 --> 00:55:19.090 align:middle line:90%
So I can write it like this.

00:55:19.090 --> 00:55:24.680 align:middle line:84%
And-- which is k equals
1 to N of u in a product,

00:55:24.680 --> 00:55:31.040 align:middle line:84%
call this vk, ek bar,
where here vk is simply

00:55:31.040 --> 00:55:46.240 align:middle line:84%
defined to be T star
ek now, let e1 up

00:55:46.240 --> 00:56:07.470 align:middle line:84%
to el be the orthonormal
subset of H obtained

00:56:07.470 --> 00:56:20.530 align:middle line:84%
by applying the Gram-Schmidt
process to vectors E1, e2

00:56:20.530 --> 00:56:26.290 align:middle line:90%
up to eL vL, up to vL.

00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:31.210 align:middle line:84%
So applying it to this subset,
I keep the e1 up to eL's.

00:56:31.210 --> 00:56:32.740 align:middle line:84%
And then I might
pick up some more

00:56:32.740 --> 00:56:35.380 align:middle line:84%
from some more
orthonormal vectors

00:56:35.380 --> 00:56:39.160 align:middle line:84%
that are the normal
to these guys when

00:56:39.160 --> 00:56:40.480 align:middle line:90%
I hit the v1's up to vl.

00:56:40.480 --> 00:56:44.020 align:middle line:90%


00:56:44.020 --> 00:56:47.120 align:middle line:84%
I should have gone
to N, I'm sorry.

00:56:47.120 --> 00:56:55.110 align:middle line:90%
So not L here, but N.

00:56:55.110 --> 00:56:58.440 align:middle line:84%
So I obtained an
orthonormal subset

00:56:58.440 --> 00:57:02.740 align:middle line:84%
that spans the same
span of these guys.

00:57:02.740 --> 00:57:05.910 align:middle line:90%
And so what does that mean?

00:57:05.910 --> 00:57:09.940 align:middle line:90%


00:57:09.940 --> 00:57:20.590 align:middle line:84%
Then there exists
numbers akj, bkj--

00:57:20.590 --> 00:57:21.430 align:middle line:90%
let's call it i--

00:57:21.430 --> 00:57:25.460 align:middle line:90%


00:57:25.460 --> 00:57:33.180 align:middle line:84%
such that ek bar is in the span
of these orthonormal vectors.

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:44.500 align:middle line:84%
So I can write it as
aki, ei k equals 1 to l--

00:57:44.500 --> 00:57:46.990 align:middle line:84%
no, no, no, sums
going the wrong way--

00:57:46.990 --> 00:57:58.550 align:middle line:84%
i equals 1 to l, and also
the same for the v's, some vk

00:57:58.550 --> 00:58:09.500 align:middle line:90%
equals j equals 1 to l bkj kj.

00:58:09.500 --> 00:58:14.810 align:middle line:84%
So these are obtained by the
Gram-Schmidt process applied

00:58:14.810 --> 00:58:16.280 align:middle line:90%
to this collection of vectors.

00:58:16.280 --> 00:58:18.890 align:middle line:84%
And therefore I can write
each one of these vectors

00:58:18.890 --> 00:58:22.020 align:middle line:84%
as a linear combination of
these orthonormal vectors.

00:58:22.020 --> 00:58:25.580 align:middle line:90%
So that's all I'm saying here.

00:58:25.580 --> 00:58:32.110 align:middle line:84%
Now we just plug this
in to this computation.

00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:36.040 align:middle line:84%
And we have Tu which we
computed was equal to sum from k

00:58:36.040 --> 00:58:42.280 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to N of u in
a product vk, ek bar.

00:58:42.280 --> 00:58:43.540 align:middle line:90%
This is equal to--

00:58:43.540 --> 00:58:47.360 align:middle line:84%
now if I stick in
these sums here,

00:58:47.360 --> 00:58:53.080 align:middle line:84%
I get sum ij equals
1 to L of some k

00:58:53.080 --> 00:59:06.290 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to N of aki,
bkj complex conjugate.

00:59:06.290 --> 00:59:12.040 align:middle line:90%


00:59:12.040 --> 00:59:14.175 align:middle line:90%
Now, u in a product ej--

00:59:14.175 --> 00:59:18.610 align:middle line:90%


00:59:18.610 --> 00:59:21.970 align:middle line:84%
this is coming from when
I stick in the vk's--

00:59:21.970 --> 00:59:23.260 align:middle line:90%
ei.

00:59:23.260 --> 00:59:24.760 align:middle line:84%
And this is coming
from when I stick

00:59:24.760 --> 00:59:29.200 align:middle line:90%
in what I have for the ek bar.

00:59:29.200 --> 00:59:33.430 align:middle line:84%
And this number right
here is my number cij.

00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:38.370 align:middle line:90%


00:59:38.370 --> 00:59:39.240 align:middle line:90%
And that's the end.

00:59:39.240 --> 00:59:44.370 align:middle line:84%
So finite rank operators
are really just matrices.

00:59:44.370 --> 00:59:46.350 align:middle line:84%
You can just compute
these numbers

00:59:46.350 --> 00:59:50.730 align:middle line:84%
and they completely characterize
the linear operator.

00:59:50.730 --> 00:59:55.760 align:middle line:90%


00:59:55.760 --> 00:59:59.350 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, the
kernel, the null space of T

00:59:59.350 --> 01:00:06.370 align:middle line:84%
is the subspace that's
orthogonal to the e1 up to eL,

01:00:06.370 --> 01:00:07.690 align:middle line:90%
or at least includes that.

01:00:07.690 --> 01:00:20.660 align:middle line:90%


01:00:20.660 --> 01:00:31.670 align:middle line:84%
Now from this we can conclude a
nice property about finite rank

01:00:31.670 --> 01:00:36.260 align:middle line:84%
operators, namely the first
is that if T is a finite rank

01:00:36.260 --> 01:00:40.160 align:middle line:84%
operator, then its adjoint is
also a finite rank operator.

01:00:40.160 --> 01:00:47.200 align:middle line:90%


01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:53.790 align:middle line:84%
And if T is a
finite rank operator

01:00:53.790 --> 01:01:02.280 align:middle line:84%
and A and B are just bounded
linear operators on H, then

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:09.520 align:middle line:84%
A times T times B, this is
also a finite rank operator.

01:01:09.520 --> 01:01:12.640 align:middle line:84%
In fancy or in
short form you would

01:01:12.640 --> 01:01:20.590 align:middle line:84%
say that this is a star closed
two-sided ideal in the space

01:01:20.590 --> 01:01:26.110 align:middle line:84%
of bounded linear operators, or
its closed undertaking adjoints

01:01:26.110 --> 01:01:29.590 align:middle line:84%
in closed undertaking the
two-sided multiplication

01:01:29.590 --> 01:01:33.600 align:middle line:90%
by bounded linear operators.

01:01:33.600 --> 01:01:36.910 align:middle line:90%
So 2 I'll leave as an exercise.

01:01:36.910 --> 01:01:42.790 align:middle line:90%


01:01:42.790 --> 01:01:45.310 align:middle line:90%
What's the point?

01:01:45.310 --> 01:01:49.270 align:middle line:84%
The T has finite
dimensional range.

01:01:49.270 --> 01:01:52.270 align:middle line:84%
A then acts on that
finite dimensional range.

01:01:52.270 --> 01:01:56.970 align:middle line:84%
And therefore its range,
the range of A hitting T

01:01:56.970 --> 01:01:59.130 align:middle line:90%
must then be finite dimensional.

01:01:59.130 --> 01:02:03.200 align:middle line:84%
And what you do with B
really doesn't matter.

01:02:03.200 --> 01:02:05.150 align:middle line:90%
So 1 is an exercise.

01:02:05.150 --> 01:02:05.905 align:middle line:90%
So let's prove 1.

01:02:05.905 --> 01:02:10.690 align:middle line:84%
1 we'll prove using this
characterization of finite rink

01:02:10.690 --> 01:02:11.810 align:middle line:90%
operators.

01:02:11.810 --> 01:02:17.470 align:middle line:84%
So we're assuming
T is finite rank.

01:02:17.470 --> 01:02:29.650 align:middle line:84%
So we can write T as some
constant cij, u and a product

01:02:29.650 --> 01:02:37.530 align:middle line:84%
ej, ei, u in H. So we can
express it in that way

01:02:37.530 --> 01:02:41.680 align:middle line:84%
where these are just some fixed
constants not depending on u.

01:02:41.680 --> 01:02:46.950 align:middle line:84%
Now let's compute how
T star x on vectors

01:02:46.950 --> 01:02:50.280 align:middle line:84%
by using this defining
property of the adjoint.

01:02:50.280 --> 01:02:53.410 align:middle line:90%


01:02:53.410 --> 01:02:56.720 align:middle line:90%
Then u, T star v--

01:02:56.720 --> 01:02:58.720 align:middle line:84%
what we're going to do
is we're going to come up

01:02:58.720 --> 01:03:03.700 align:middle line:84%
with an expression for this
as u in a product something

01:03:03.700 --> 01:03:06.820 align:middle line:84%
and from which we can conclude
that T star v has to equal

01:03:06.820 --> 01:03:12.040 align:middle line:90%
that something for all v.

01:03:12.040 --> 01:03:16.120 align:middle line:84%
Now this is equal
to, by the defining

01:03:16.120 --> 01:03:20.980 align:middle line:84%
property of the adjoint,
T applied to u, v. So this

01:03:20.980 --> 01:03:21.625 align:middle line:90%
is equal to--

01:03:21.625 --> 01:03:25.840 align:middle line:90%


01:03:25.840 --> 01:03:28.300 align:middle line:84%
i and j will be understood
to go from 1 to l.

01:03:28.300 --> 01:03:31.960 align:middle line:84%
So this is just finite,
a finite sum, OK--

01:03:31.960 --> 01:03:39.590 align:middle line:90%
cij, u, ei-- or ej, sorry--

01:03:39.590 --> 01:03:42.850 align:middle line:90%
ei, inner product v.

01:03:42.850 --> 01:03:50.000 align:middle line:84%
Now taking the inner product
is linear in the first entry.

01:03:50.000 --> 01:03:52.070 align:middle line:90%
So this is equal to sum ij.

01:03:52.070 --> 01:03:58.530 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is a finite sum
ij, u, ej, inner product

01:03:58.530 --> 01:04:08.990 align:middle line:84%
ei, v. And now I'm just going
to switch what I'm summing.

01:04:08.990 --> 01:04:25.880 align:middle line:84%
This I can write as now u
inner product sum ij cij bar.

01:04:25.880 --> 01:04:28.100 align:middle line:90%
So if I want to--

01:04:28.100 --> 01:04:41.430 align:middle line:84%
this I can write as bar, bar,
but ei, v, complex conjugate,

01:04:41.430 --> 01:04:41.930 align:middle line:90%
ej.

01:04:41.930 --> 01:04:46.350 align:middle line:90%


01:04:46.350 --> 01:04:48.570 align:middle line:84%
We can check this
as correct because--

01:04:48.570 --> 01:04:50.640 align:middle line:84%
so u will then inner
product with each

01:04:50.640 --> 01:04:53.070 align:middle line:84%
of the ej's and these
constants when they come out

01:04:53.070 --> 01:04:56.310 align:middle line:84%
of the inner product, get hit
by a complex conjugate, which

01:04:56.310 --> 01:05:01.190 align:middle line:84%
turns them back
into cij and ei, v.

01:05:01.190 --> 01:05:05.630 align:middle line:84%
Now, the complex conjugate
of the inner product

01:05:05.630 --> 01:05:11.370 align:middle line:84%
is just the same as
if I flip the entry.

01:05:11.370 --> 01:05:19.235 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to
cij times v, ei, ej.

01:05:19.235 --> 01:05:24.070 align:middle line:90%


01:05:24.070 --> 01:05:27.040 align:middle line:84%
Now, this holds for all u
and v. So what have I shown?

01:05:27.040 --> 01:05:34.570 align:middle line:84%
I've shown that u T star v minus
this finite sum, so I actually

01:05:34.570 --> 01:05:46.210 align:middle line:84%
put it in here, cij v, ei,
vj, this equals 0 for all u,

01:05:46.210 --> 01:05:48.190 align:middle line:90%
for all v.

01:05:48.190 --> 01:05:51.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, for a fixed v
this quantity here

01:05:51.790 --> 01:05:56.410 align:middle line:84%
is orthogonal to everything
in H. So it has to be 0.

01:05:56.410 --> 01:05:58.300 align:middle line:84%
And therefore I
conclude that T star

01:05:58.300 --> 01:06:04.690 align:middle line:84%
v has to equal sum
from ij equals 1 to l,

01:06:04.690 --> 01:06:11.350 align:middle line:84%
the complex conjugates of
cij, v inner product ei,

01:06:11.350 --> 01:06:16.450 align:middle line:84%
ej for all v in
H. And that proves

01:06:16.450 --> 01:06:24.350 align:middle line:84%
that T star is a
finite rank, and also

01:06:24.350 --> 01:06:28.220 align:middle line:84%
gives you the expression for
the coefficients in terms

01:06:28.220 --> 01:06:29.150 align:middle line:90%
of the old ones.

01:06:29.150 --> 01:06:32.120 align:middle line:90%
I could write this--

01:06:32.120 --> 01:06:36.110 align:middle line:84%
now if I wanted to write
it in terms of the i

01:06:36.110 --> 01:06:39.740 align:middle line:84%
being in front of the bases
vector that I'm having here,

01:06:39.740 --> 01:06:42.254 align:middle line:90%
I write it as sum ij.

01:06:42.254 --> 01:06:48.870 align:middle line:90%


01:06:48.870 --> 01:06:52.920 align:middle line:84%
So we see that the matrix
corresponding to the adjoint is

01:06:52.920 --> 01:06:55.830 align:middle line:84%
what we computed
last class basically,

01:06:55.830 --> 01:07:01.080 align:middle line:84%
is the complex conjugate of
the transpose of this matrix,

01:07:01.080 --> 01:07:02.390 align:middle line:90%
of the original matrix cij's.

01:07:02.390 --> 01:07:06.520 align:middle line:90%


01:07:06.520 --> 01:07:09.775 align:middle line:84%
All right, so that proves
adjoints are also finite rank.

01:07:09.775 --> 01:07:13.330 align:middle line:90%


01:07:13.330 --> 01:07:25.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, so taking the
adjoint of a finite rank,

01:07:25.270 --> 01:07:31.570 align:middle line:84%
operator leaves you in the
space of finite rank operators.

01:07:31.570 --> 01:07:34.750 align:middle line:84%
If you take finite
rank operators

01:07:34.750 --> 01:07:37.750 align:middle line:84%
and compose them on the
left and on the right

01:07:37.750 --> 01:07:42.295 align:middle line:84%
with bounded operators,
you remain finite rank.

01:07:42.295 --> 01:07:47.610 align:middle line:90%


01:07:47.610 --> 01:07:49.470 align:middle line:84%
The subset of finite
rank operators

01:07:49.470 --> 01:07:54.600 align:middle line:84%
also form a subspace
in the Banach space

01:07:54.600 --> 01:07:58.830 align:middle line:84%
of bounded linear operators
on a Hilbert space.

01:07:58.830 --> 01:08:01.650 align:middle line:84%
So the space of bounded linear
operators on a Hilbert space

01:08:01.650 --> 01:08:07.620 align:middle line:84%
naturally come with a norm
as well, the operator norm.

01:08:07.620 --> 01:08:17.109 align:middle line:84%
So the next obvious question is,
is the subspace of finite rank

01:08:17.109 --> 01:08:18.990 align:middle line:90%
operators, is this closed?

01:08:18.990 --> 01:08:38.490 align:middle line:90%


01:08:38.490 --> 01:08:41.979 align:middle line:84%
Closed not meaning-- not
as a closed undertaking

01:08:41.979 --> 01:08:42.939 align:middle line:90%
linear combinations.

01:08:42.939 --> 01:08:44.606 align:middle line:84%
Of course that holds
for every subspace.

01:08:44.606 --> 01:08:56.770 align:middle line:84%
But if I take a sequence
of finite rank operators

01:08:56.770 --> 01:09:11.460 align:middle line:84%
that are converging in
operator norm to something,

01:09:11.460 --> 01:09:18.200 align:middle line:84%
then is the limit also
a finite rank operator?

01:09:18.200 --> 01:09:23.090 align:middle line:84%
So that's the definition
of being a closed subspace,

01:09:23.090 --> 01:09:29.830 align:middle line:84%
or let me say subset so
that we're now asking

01:09:29.830 --> 01:09:31.870 align:middle line:90%
about this in a metric sense.

01:09:31.870 --> 01:09:37.210 align:middle line:84%
Is it a closed
subset in the space

01:09:37.210 --> 01:09:39.290 align:middle line:90%
of bounded linear operators?

01:09:39.290 --> 01:09:40.749 align:middle line:90%
Now, the answer to this is no.

01:09:40.749 --> 01:09:45.540 align:middle line:90%


01:09:45.540 --> 01:09:48.000 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:54.180 align:middle line:90%
Let's, for example, take Tn.

01:09:54.180 --> 01:09:59.280 align:middle line:84%
So this will be a sequence
of operators from little l2

01:09:59.280 --> 01:10:01.740 align:middle line:90%
to little l2.

01:10:01.740 --> 01:10:12.610 align:middle line:84%
And for an element
a in little l2,

01:10:12.610 --> 01:10:14.920 align:middle line:84%
this should give me another
sequence in little l2.

01:10:14.920 --> 01:10:19.720 align:middle line:84%
And it'll be the sequence
given by a1 over 1, a2 over 2,

01:10:19.720 --> 01:10:26.290 align:middle line:84%
up to an over n, and
then zeros after that.

01:10:26.290 --> 01:10:38.780 align:middle line:84%
All right, so T1,
the operator T1

01:10:38.780 --> 01:10:41.210 align:middle line:84%
takes the sequence in
little l2 and just spits out

01:10:41.210 --> 01:10:44.180 align:middle line:84%
the sequence that has the
first entry divided by 1.

01:10:44.180 --> 01:10:45.950 align:middle line:90%
And 0's followed after that.

01:10:45.950 --> 01:10:47.990 align:middle line:90%
That's the operator T1.

01:10:47.990 --> 01:10:50.840 align:middle line:84%
T2 takes a sequence
in little l2 and spits

01:10:50.840 --> 01:10:53.150 align:middle line:84%
out the first
entry divided by 1,

01:10:53.150 --> 01:11:01.970 align:middle line:84%
the second entry divided by 2,
and o's after that, T3, so on.

01:11:01.970 --> 01:11:09.260 align:middle line:90%
Now, this is just a picture.

01:11:09.260 --> 01:11:09.980 align:middle line:90%
This is formal.

01:11:09.980 --> 01:11:12.050 align:middle line:84%
This is not meant
to mean anything.

01:11:12.050 --> 01:11:15.590 align:middle line:84%
If I were to express this
as an infinite matrix

01:11:15.590 --> 01:11:18.830 align:middle line:84%
times an infinite vector, or
infinite length vector where

01:11:18.830 --> 01:11:22.400 align:middle line:84%
the infinite length is
the sequence ak's, this

01:11:22.400 --> 01:11:33.100 align:middle line:84%
is 1, 1/2, 1/3, 1
over n, 0, 0, 0, 0.

01:11:33.100 --> 01:11:54.130 align:middle line:84%
This infinite matrix times
a1, a2, a3, and so on.

01:11:54.130 --> 01:12:00.810 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just multiplying a1
by 1, a2 by 1/2, a3 by a 1/3,

01:12:00.810 --> 01:12:06.150 align:middle line:84%
and then up to a n over n,
multiplying a n by 1 over n.

01:12:06.150 --> 01:12:09.720 align:middle line:84%
And then the rest of the entries
get spat out to give me 0.

01:12:09.720 --> 01:12:12.960 align:middle line:84%
So these are the
finite rank operators.

01:12:12.960 --> 01:12:21.870 align:middle line:90%


01:12:21.870 --> 01:12:36.930 align:middle line:84%
And you can check that the
Tn minus T in operator norm

01:12:36.930 --> 01:12:42.240 align:middle line:90%
goes to 0, where what is T?

01:12:42.240 --> 01:12:45.990 align:middle line:84%
T is the operator that
takes a spits out--

01:12:45.990 --> 01:12:53.320 align:middle line:84%
so a will be an
element in little l2.

01:12:53.320 --> 01:12:55.780 align:middle line:84%
Now as n goes to
infinity, you can kind of

01:12:55.780 --> 01:12:58.720 align:middle line:84%
guess what's going to happen is
I end up multiplying everything

01:12:58.720 --> 01:13:03.730 align:middle line:84%
by 1 over wherever
entry I'm at, a1 over 1,

01:13:03.730 --> 01:13:10.600 align:middle line:84%
a2 over 2, a3 over 3, a4
over 4, and so on, and on.

01:13:10.600 --> 01:13:15.390 align:middle line:90%


01:13:15.390 --> 01:13:20.600 align:middle line:84%
So as an exercise you can
show that the operator

01:13:20.600 --> 01:13:22.850 align:middle line:84%
norm of T minus Tn,
this is less than

01:13:22.850 --> 01:13:30.180 align:middle line:84%
or equal to v1 over n plus
1, something like that, OK.

01:13:30.180 --> 01:13:34.880 align:middle line:90%


01:13:34.880 --> 01:13:39.620 align:middle line:84%
Now, this operator here
does not have finite rank

01:13:39.620 --> 01:13:52.920 align:middle line:84%
because then I can find
infinitely many linearly

01:13:52.920 --> 01:13:54.360 align:middle line:84%
independent vectors
in the range.

01:13:54.360 --> 01:13:58.590 align:middle line:84%
For example T of e1
where this here is

01:13:58.590 --> 01:14:01.580 align:middle line:90%
the basis vector gives me the--

01:14:01.580 --> 01:14:07.760 align:middle line:90%


01:14:07.760 --> 01:14:10.340 align:middle line:84%
this is the first basis
vector in little l2.

01:14:10.340 --> 01:14:13.130 align:middle line:84%
If this is the second
basis vector in little l2,

01:14:13.130 --> 01:14:13.955 align:middle line:90%
this is just--

01:14:13.955 --> 01:14:20.470 align:middle line:90%


01:14:20.470 --> 01:14:21.500 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

01:14:21.500 --> 01:14:26.690 align:middle line:90%
If I applied T to en, This.

01:14:26.690 --> 01:14:30.560 align:middle line:90%
Is 1 over n, 0, 0, and so on.

01:14:30.560 --> 01:14:31.760 align:middle line:90%
This is in the n spot.

01:14:31.760 --> 01:14:36.040 align:middle line:90%


01:14:36.040 --> 01:14:39.720 align:middle line:84%
So it just takes each
basis vector and--

01:14:39.720 --> 01:14:47.070 align:middle line:84%
so if you like, this is e1, this
is 1/2 e2, this is 1 over n en,

01:14:47.070 --> 01:14:52.560 align:middle line:84%
and multiplies each of these
basis vectors by 1 over

01:14:52.560 --> 01:14:56.600 align:middle line:90%
the integer that's--

01:14:56.600 --> 01:15:00.080 align:middle line:90%


01:15:00.080 --> 01:15:04.080 align:middle line:84%
not delineating--
denoting, I'm sorry.

01:15:04.080 --> 01:15:11.990 align:middle line:84%
All right, it's the end of
a day so some of my thinking

01:15:11.990 --> 01:15:12.680 align:middle line:90%
has slowed down.

01:15:12.680 --> 01:15:15.230 align:middle line:90%


01:15:15.230 --> 01:15:19.010 align:middle line:84%
So finite rank operators
are not closed--

01:15:19.010 --> 01:15:22.580 align:middle line:84%
is not a closed
subset of the space

01:15:22.580 --> 01:15:24.770 align:middle line:90%
of bounded linear operators.

01:15:24.770 --> 01:15:27.870 align:middle line:90%
So what's the closure?

01:15:27.870 --> 01:15:32.120 align:middle line:90%


01:15:32.120 --> 01:15:34.520 align:middle line:84%
And finite rank operators
we'd like to think we

01:15:34.520 --> 01:15:36.770 align:middle line:90%
know how to solve equations for.

01:15:36.770 --> 01:15:41.690 align:middle line:84%
I mean, just based on this
expression for a finite rank

01:15:41.690 --> 01:15:48.630 align:middle line:84%
operator, well, if we want to
solve Tu equals v, well, first

01:15:48.630 --> 01:15:55.240 align:middle line:84%
we know v has to be in the
range of or in the span

01:15:55.240 --> 01:15:57.610 align:middle line:84%
of these basis vectors
that appear here.

01:15:57.610 --> 01:16:00.610 align:middle line:84%
And now once we've
restricted to that,

01:16:00.610 --> 01:16:05.620 align:middle line:84%
then we can solve the equation
by looking at the properties

01:16:05.620 --> 01:16:06.550 align:middle line:90%
of this matrix.

01:16:06.550 --> 01:16:09.880 align:middle line:84%
And the null space will
be not only the null space

01:16:09.880 --> 01:16:12.730 align:middle line:84%
that sits inside the span
of these basis vectors,

01:16:12.730 --> 01:16:16.870 align:middle line:84%
but it will also include
the orthogonal complement

01:16:16.870 --> 01:16:20.170 align:middle line:90%
in H of these basis vectors.

01:16:20.170 --> 01:16:25.950 align:middle line:84%
So solving equations involving
finite rank operators

01:16:25.950 --> 01:16:29.490 align:middle line:84%
ought to be fairly
straightforward

01:16:29.490 --> 01:16:30.785 align:middle line:90%
if we remember linear algebra.

01:16:30.785 --> 01:16:34.050 align:middle line:90%


01:16:34.050 --> 01:16:37.847 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, they're
not closed upon taking

01:16:37.847 --> 01:16:38.930 align:middle line:90%
some subsequential limits.

01:16:38.930 --> 01:16:44.750 align:middle line:84%
But we can identify
the closure of--

01:16:44.750 --> 01:16:54.440 align:middle line:84%
so now the question
is, what is the closure

01:16:54.440 --> 01:16:59.990 align:middle line:84%
of finite rank
operators in the space

01:16:59.990 --> 01:17:02.060 align:middle line:84%
of bounded linear
operators on H?

01:17:02.060 --> 01:17:04.640 align:middle line:84%
So something in the closure has
to be a limit of finite rank

01:17:04.640 --> 01:17:05.510 align:middle line:90%
operators.

01:17:05.510 --> 01:17:09.140 align:middle line:84%
And since we know
how to solve finite--

01:17:09.140 --> 01:17:11.150 align:middle line:84%
equations involving
finite rank operators,

01:17:11.150 --> 01:17:13.670 align:middle line:84%
hopefully we know how
to solve equations use

01:17:13.670 --> 01:17:15.980 align:middle line:90%
with these operators as well.

01:17:15.980 --> 01:17:18.440 align:middle line:84%
Is there a simple way
we can characterize--

01:17:18.440 --> 01:17:23.160 align:middle line:84%
or not simple, but at
least a more useful way

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:26.430 align:middle line:84%
to characterize them than just
being the limit of finite rank

01:17:26.430 --> 01:17:27.330 align:middle line:90%
operators?

01:17:27.330 --> 01:17:30.810 align:middle line:90%


01:17:30.810 --> 01:17:33.030 align:middle line:84%
So the answer to
this question is--

01:17:33.030 --> 01:17:34.290 align:middle line:90%
so I won't even write--

01:17:34.290 --> 01:17:36.360 align:middle line:90%
OK, I'll write answer--

01:17:36.360 --> 01:17:40.800 align:middle line:84%
is what are called
compact operators, so

01:17:40.800 --> 01:17:42.810 align:middle line:84%
the subspace of
compact operators.

01:17:42.810 --> 01:17:48.340 align:middle line:90%


01:17:48.340 --> 01:17:50.780 align:middle line:90%
So let me make a definition.

01:17:50.780 --> 01:18:06.040 align:middle line:84%
So k, a bounded linear
operator is compact operator

01:18:06.040 --> 01:18:24.770 align:middle line:84%
if the closure of the image
of the closed unit ball in H--

01:18:24.770 --> 01:18:28.340 align:middle line:84%
so the closure of
the image by k--

01:18:28.340 --> 01:18:30.590 align:middle line:84%
or the closure of the image
of the closed unit ball

01:18:30.590 --> 01:18:31.145 align:middle line:90%
is compact.

01:18:31.145 --> 01:18:35.010 align:middle line:90%


01:18:35.010 --> 01:18:41.520 align:middle line:84%
I can also write this as a
closure of the subset ku norm

01:18:41.520 --> 01:18:43.140 align:middle line:90%
of u less than or equal to 1.

01:18:43.140 --> 01:18:51.550 align:middle line:90%


01:18:51.550 --> 01:18:56.520 align:middle line:84%
So I won't have time to prove
this theorem in this class,

01:18:56.520 --> 01:18:58.440 align:middle line:90%
but we'll do it next time.

01:18:58.440 --> 01:19:02.200 align:middle line:84%
And we'll actually
show that, in fact,

01:19:02.200 --> 01:19:17.430 align:middle line:84%
this class of compact operators
which are defined in this way,

01:19:17.430 --> 01:19:21.930 align:middle line:84%
this is a compact operator
according to this definition

01:19:21.930 --> 01:19:31.750 align:middle line:84%
if and only if k is in
the closure of finite rank

01:19:31.750 --> 01:19:34.570 align:middle line:84%
operators, meaning there exists
a sequence of finite rank

01:19:34.570 --> 01:19:38.920 align:middle line:84%
operators converging
in operator norm to k.

01:19:38.920 --> 01:19:41.350 align:middle line:84%
And, again, why
are we interested

01:19:41.350 --> 01:19:44.830 align:middle line:90%
in compact operators?

01:19:44.830 --> 01:19:47.320 align:middle line:84%
Well, we know how
to solve finite rank

01:19:47.320 --> 01:19:49.990 align:middle line:84%
or equations involving
finite rank operators.

01:19:49.990 --> 01:19:57.040 align:middle line:84%
We just look at the subspace
which that contains the range.

01:19:57.040 --> 01:20:01.120 align:middle line:84%
And then we look at the
matrix that appears there

01:20:01.120 --> 01:20:03.870 align:middle line:90%
in that decomposition.

01:20:03.870 --> 01:20:11.580 align:middle line:84%
Compact operators there are
something completely new.

01:20:11.580 --> 01:20:16.620 align:middle line:84%
When we move from finite
dimensional linear algebra

01:20:16.620 --> 01:20:20.850 align:middle line:84%
now to functional
analysis because these

01:20:20.850 --> 01:20:25.740 align:middle line:84%
are not exactly necessarily
equal to finite rank operators.

01:20:25.740 --> 01:20:27.600 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we just did
this example a minute

01:20:27.600 --> 01:20:34.090 align:middle line:84%
ago of this operator
given here by where

01:20:34.090 --> 01:20:38.440 align:middle line:84%
I take the entry of the sequence
and divide it by the place

01:20:38.440 --> 01:20:42.400 align:middle line:84%
that it occurred in the sequence
for a sequence given in l2.

01:20:42.400 --> 01:20:47.230 align:middle line:84%
So if you believe the theorem,
this is a finite rank--

01:20:47.230 --> 01:20:48.040 align:middle line:90%
I mean, not finite.

01:20:48.040 --> 01:20:49.630 align:middle line:90%
This is a compact operator.

01:20:49.630 --> 01:20:56.940 align:middle line:84%
It's the limit of this sequence
of finite rank operators.

01:20:56.940 --> 01:21:01.170 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps you want to
take a try at trying

01:21:01.170 --> 01:21:03.090 align:middle line:84%
to prove this as
a compact operator

01:21:03.090 --> 01:21:07.140 align:middle line:84%
directly from the
definition, or not.

01:21:07.140 --> 01:21:10.710 align:middle line:84%
you can wait till next time
and just use this theorem.

01:21:10.710 --> 01:21:15.110 align:middle line:84%
But many other operators,
many interesting operators,

01:21:15.110 --> 01:21:18.530 align:middle line:84%
for example, inverses of
differentiable operators

01:21:18.530 --> 01:21:21.020 align:middle line:84%
in fact turn out to
be compact operators.

01:21:21.020 --> 01:21:22.880 align:middle line:84%
That's also why we're
interested in them.

01:21:22.880 --> 01:21:27.440 align:middle line:90%


01:21:27.440 --> 01:21:32.480 align:middle line:84%
So we got these by taking
the closure of finite rank

01:21:32.480 --> 01:21:33.170 align:middle line:90%
operators.

01:21:33.170 --> 01:21:39.490 align:middle line:84%
And then after-- so now we
have this interesting subspace

01:21:39.490 --> 01:21:43.720 align:middle line:84%
of operators that we hope to
be able to solve equations

01:21:43.720 --> 01:21:44.850 align:middle line:90%
involving.

01:21:44.850 --> 01:21:50.630 align:middle line:84%
And that'll be basically
what takes up the remainder

01:21:50.630 --> 01:21:52.385 align:middle line:90%
of our time in this course.

01:21:52.385 --> 01:21:56.500 align:middle line:90%


01:21:56.500 --> 01:21:59.870 align:middle line:84%
So you just staring
at this definition,

01:21:59.870 --> 01:22:01.240 align:middle line:90%
you can kind of see that--

01:22:01.240 --> 01:22:05.350 align:middle line:84%
well, you can see that if k
is a finite rank operator,

01:22:05.350 --> 01:22:07.390 align:middle line:90%
then it's a compact operator.

01:22:07.390 --> 01:22:10.630 align:middle line:84%
So we're not going to prove this
theorem, like I said, but just

01:22:10.630 --> 01:22:17.390 align:middle line:84%
to see or do a sanity check
that finite rank operators ought

01:22:17.390 --> 01:22:18.590 align:middle line:90%
to be compact operators.

01:22:18.590 --> 01:22:24.830 align:middle line:84%
Well, a finite rank
operator, the image of this

01:22:24.830 --> 01:22:28.400 align:middle line:84%
closed unit ball of a finite
by a finite rank operator,

01:22:28.400 --> 01:22:32.240 align:middle line:84%
that's going to be
a bounded subset

01:22:32.240 --> 01:22:35.540 align:middle line:84%
of a finite
dimensional subspace.

01:22:35.540 --> 01:22:39.260 align:middle line:84%
Any bounded operator takes
bounded sets to bounded sets.

01:22:39.260 --> 01:22:43.880 align:middle line:84%
So if it's finite rank,
it takes this bounded set

01:22:43.880 --> 01:22:51.270 align:middle line:84%
to a bounded subset of a finite
dimensional subspace of H.

01:22:51.270 --> 01:22:52.470 align:middle line:90%
And then I take the closure.

01:22:52.470 --> 01:22:55.770 align:middle line:84%
I get a bounded
and closed subset

01:22:55.770 --> 01:22:58.120 align:middle line:84%
of a finite
dimensional subspace.

01:22:58.120 --> 01:23:04.290 align:middle line:84%
And we know by Heine-Borel
that closed and bounded subsets

01:23:04.290 --> 01:23:07.650 align:middle line:84%
of finite dimensional
subspaces are compact.

01:23:07.650 --> 01:23:11.100 align:middle line:84%
So that shows why
at the very minimum

01:23:11.100 --> 01:23:13.830 align:middle line:84%
you should believe that finite
rank operators are compact.

01:23:13.830 --> 01:23:16.430 align:middle line:90%
OK, so we'll stop there.

01:23:16.430 --> 01:23:22.000 align:middle line:90%